WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.433
Data locality begins with the question a user asks,
but it must also account for how facts change.

00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:12.866
We will use campsite reservations to distinguish
useful snapshots from duplicated information that

00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:14.835
becomes difficult to maintain.

00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:21.268
A campsite booking page needs a reservation, its
selected facilities and a short site description.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.797
Embedding a snapshot of the booked facilities can
make that page a single aggregate read.

00:00:26.797 --> 00:00:32.911
Keeping the current site description separately
avoids rewriting thousands of reservations whenever

00:00:32.911 --> 00:00:34.393
the description changes.

00:00:34.393 --> 00:00:40.446
The correct boundary depends on whether the page
promises historical booking details or the latest

00:00:40.446 --> 00:00:41.805
catalogue information.

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:45.695
Suppose 2,000 reservations contain a copied access
instruction.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.627
A correction now requires updating many copies or
accepting mixed versions during propagation.

00:00:51.627 --> 00:00:56.860
By contrast, a historical price agreed at booking
should usually remain a snapshot.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.977
Denormalization is therefore a choice about update
ownership and meaning, as well as query speed.

00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:09.156
List each embedded field, its authoritative source
and the rule for changing it before celebrating

00:01:09.156 --> 00:01:10.543
fewer network lookups.

00:01:10.543 --> 00:01:14.281
A hash gives a repeatable route from a key to a
partition.

00:01:14.281 --> 00:01:18.341
It is a routing mechanism, not a proof of balanced
performance.

00:01:18.341 --> 00:01:23.626
Let us trace one identifier and then test the
assumptions behind the distribution.

00:01:23.626 --> 00:01:29.748
Consider a toy hash space with values zero through
ninety-nine, divided into four equal ranges.

00:01:29.748 --> 00:01:35.990
A parcel identifier hashing to sixty-two is routed
to the owner of the fifty-through-seventy-four

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:36.376
range.

00:01:36.376 --> 00:01:41.010
The client needs the current ownership map as well
as the hash function.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:47.381
When ownership changes, migration and routing must
agree so requests do not silently read the wrong

00:01:47.381 --> 00:01:47.767
place.

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:52.336
Evenly distributed identifiers do not imply evenly
distributed traffic.

00:01:52.348 --> 00:01:58.578
If one parcel receives half the tracking requests,
its owner can remain overloaded while other

00:01:58.578 --> 00:01:59.638
owners are idle.

00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:04.675
Adding machines does not split that individual key’s
requests automatically.

00:02:04.675 --> 00:02:11.103
Similarly, hashes scatter nearby timestamps, so a
query for all parcels created between two times

00:02:11.103 --> 00:02:13.754
cannot simply walk adjacent hash values.

00:02:13.754 --> 00:02:19.454
Separate point lookup, range lookup and hot-key
mitigation when evaluating the design.

00:02:19.454 --> 00:02:24.708
Replication provides extra copies, but those copies
may be unable to communicate.

00:02:24.708 --> 00:02:30.806
The architectural decision becomes concrete when we
ask what the application may promise while

00:02:30.806 --> 00:02:32.363
communication is broken.

00:02:32.363 --> 00:02:35.865
Compare a rental commitment with a display
preference.

00:02:35.865 --> 00:02:40.212
A tool-rental service keeps three copies of an
availability record.

00:02:40.212 --> 00:02:43.650
A network break isolates one copy from the other
two.

00:02:43.654 --> 00:02:50.061
If both sides independently confirm rental of the
final drill, later reconciliation cannot make both

00:02:50.061 --> 00:02:51.919
promises physically possible.

00:02:51.919 --> 00:02:58.262
A design protecting that single-item invariant may
require the isolated side to refuse or delay the

00:02:58.262 --> 00:03:00.633
booking while the majority continues.

00:03:00.633 --> 00:03:06.976
A less critical observation, such as a customer’s
preferred display colour, may be accepted on both

00:03:06.976 --> 00:03:08.706
sides and reconciled later.

00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:14.613
This illustrates the CAP trade-off during a
partition: linearizable behavior and a successful

00:03:14.613 --> 00:03:19.212
response to every request at non-failing nodes
cannot both be guaranteed.

00:03:19.212 --> 00:03:23.811
It does not say replication is always unavailable or
always inconsistent.

00:03:23.811 --> 00:03:30.111
State the operation, its invariant and the failure
condition rather than assigning one slogan to the

00:03:30.111 --> 00:03:31.308
entire application.
